-- title:
--   Reversing the axis reflects the cone
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ and let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$. Writing $\tilde V(v,\vartheta)$ for the half-cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$, we have
--
--   $$\tilde V(-v, \vartheta) \;=\; -\,\tilde V(v,\vartheta),$$
--
--   where the right-hand side is the pointwise negation $\{-p : p \in \tilde V(v,\vartheta)\}$ of the set.
--
--   So the two half-cones making up a double cone are reflections of one another, and the cone construction is equivariant under the antipodal map. This is what makes the double cone $\tilde V(v,\vartheta) \cup \tilde V(-v,\vartheta)$ symmetric, and hence what makes the edge relation "$y$ lies in the cone based at $x$" behave predictably under swapping the two endpoints.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No hypotheses are imposed: $v$ need not be a unit vector and $\vartheta$ is an arbitrary real, so the identity holds even in the degenerate cases where the cone is empty or is all of $E \setminus \{0\}$. (The origin is excluded by the definition of $\tilde V$, so the cone is never all of $E$.)
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/ConeGap.lean#L158-L162

